  "facets": [
    {
      "title": "Structure and components",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "A compound-index prefix consists of consecutive fields starting at the first index field. A sort matching such a prefix can use its ordering; a filter using the index does not by itself establish sort compatibility. (high confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/sort-results-with-indexes.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:6db92bf6a737a50b255f45656b18cfacd7d1210ddffc5a49503d7ba18e6085b3",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:08c4d7c7628044aa024c8b2b431fec0260d529090499c953e1299b100ce3ab1f"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://www.percona.com/blog/mongodb-index-usage-and-mongodb-explain-part-1/",
            "https://github.com/OneUptime/blog/blob/master/posts/2026-01-30-mongodb-compound-index-strategies/README.md"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "How it works",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "A non-prefix sort subset can use index order when all preceding index fields have equality predicates. With {a:1,b:1,c:1}, find({a:5}).sort({b:1,c:1}) qualifies; omitting equality on a generally prevents that suffix from supplying global order through one scan. (high confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/sort-results-with-indexes.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:4bb17e55eed50a6dec6db527f2fdedafda3c5ba44876615e2574cf3fbb5beea3",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:f6d659a002b28bb2a864372ee6a76d103189b52c3dbb4c2809597e11a73b554a"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/equality-sort-range-guideline.md",
            "https://github.com/OneUptime/blog/blob/master/posts/2026-01-30-mongodb-compound-index-strategies/README.md"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "When an equality predicate fixes the leading field, a single remaining sort field can use either scan direction. For {type:1,timestamp:1}, find({type:\"click\"}).sort({timestamp:-1}) can use the same index as the ascending timestamp query. (high confidence) Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/sort-results-with-indexes.md",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/sort-results-with-indexes.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:d63d98fe9fc23d222e78ba1f480641b2657b2cd51d3c658cfdeda66ce83b9c6b",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:78fef9912933efb20d016559e262017d71ccf9e2d29db4592b494869a01ef2a3"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://github.com/OneUptime/blog/blob/master/posts/2026-01-30-mongodb-compound-index-strategies/README.md"
          ],
          "remediation": {
