    },
    {
      "title": "Problems, failure modes and limitations",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "For an unconstrained compound sort, sort fields must occur in index field order. An index {a:1,b:-1} can provide {a:1,b:-1} or {a:-1,b:1}, but cannot provide {a:1,b:1}, {a:-1,b:-1}, or the reordered sort {b:-1,a:1}. (high confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/sort-results-with-indexes.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:0b818863858f862be4403a426a80c0665acb7938a107646d39c569d34f30d767",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:ded25780170ae30c4ad577b2b501166e80c1dff8360fa0113b250a4169af2a2e"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/indexes/index-types/index-compound/sort-order.md",
            "https://github.com/OneUptime/blog/blob/master/posts/2026-01-30-mongodb-compound-index-strategies/README.md",
            "https://www.percona.com/blog/mongodb-index-usage-and-mongodb-explain-part-1/"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparisons and alternatives",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "For sort preservation, equality fields generally precede sort fields, and range fields follow them. A highly selective range can instead justify ERS and a subsequent blocking sort, so ESR is a guideline rather than a guarantee of the fastest plan. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/equality-sort-range-guideline.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:2348a591ca5298cb6f98c80f02119275fa994ef067d2243e3e2be3f98c69da27",
          "validationStatus": "NOT-SAMPLED",
          "evidenceIds": [],
          "also": [
            "https://github.com/OneUptime/blog/blob/master/posts/2026-01-30-mongodb-compound-index-strategies/README.md"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Facts and statements",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "A compound index's B-tree stores sorted data in the order the index specifies its fields. The order of the indexed fields affects the index's effectiveness. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/indexes/index-types/index-compound.md",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/indexes/index-types/index-compound.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:cd44fc6c406767fd3b160e8647012a2c0806612daac238818c88b535822f6ffa",
          "validationStatus": "NOT-SAMPLED",
          "evidenceIds": [],
          "also": [
            "https://www.percona.com/blog/want-mongodb-performance-you-will-need-to-add-and-remove-indexes/",
            "https://github.com/OneUptime/blog/blob/master/posts/2026-01-30-mongodb-compound-index-strategies/README.md"
          ],
          "remediation": {
            "claim_id": "clm:b9b2875d83c46fcf1607d152adb6377d75718740528e52d34181b3872f57e508",
            "outcome": "CORRECTED",
            "at": "2026-11-02T09:02:43Z"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "A leading index prefix can supply sort order without equality predicates on those sort fields; for example, {a:1,b:1,c:1,d:1} supports sorting by {a:1,b:1} or {a:-1,b:-1}. (high confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/sort-results-with-indexes.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:31134e08f159cff6b39f825287de0956038a346d4341440bd3fd855f1ab7cae6",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:2db6290ad2b6b066bf0f6c3a36637a79fa18e231b3289ad5932708d87d90653e"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/indexes/index-types/index-compound/sort-order.md"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A range predicate does not universally prevent index-provided sorting. With {a:1,b:1,c:1,d:1}, find({a:{$gt:4}}).sort({a:1,b:1}) preserves index order; a range on an omitted preceding field does not generally provide global order for a later sort field. (high confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/sort-results-with-indexes.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:1089791b1340ab90c0acc66edc1ebbe24e472a589fc79da0b0bfd517ee96c8f4",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:2f1417076fec0e6138a965334261866c7320909f345885962c8bd656302cc1de"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/equality-sort-range-guideline.md"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Small $in lists (fewer than 201 elements) used with sort are expanded and merged in the index sort order with a SORT_MERGE stage, similar to an equality predicate. Lists of 201 or more elements are ordered like a range operator, so subsequent index fields cannot be used for sorting. The 201 threshold is not guaranteed to stay the same across MongoDB versions. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/equality-sort-range-guideline.md",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/equality-sort-range-guideline.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:85f02a416e50cd902a6c170adaa9f61a82e103635585dba6d8fd702a7a6c4795",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:2d8f4daca17b566616542b256da162181707acf7da8c5656f5b352c7dbe9fd75",
            "ev:2dda8d38084e6089af7823cb262425f59ec2bfdf02669c9772915963fc9f9903"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://github.com/OneUptime/blog/blob/master/posts/2026-01-30-mongodb-compound-index-strategies/README.md"
          ],
          "remediation": {
            "claim_id": "clm:4bc1420563012ffd97028d2733a0c16dc24919cee55521d29d68b7e7799d1f3d",
            "outcome": "CORRECTED",
            "at": "2026-11-02T09:02:43Z"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "SERVER-14070 records a historical planner defect where {a:1,b:1} and find({a:1,b:1}).sort({b:1}) unnecessarily required sorting. The issue lists fixes in 2.6.11 and 2.8.0-rc3; this is historical evidence, not a claim that modern versions retain the bug. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://jira.mongodb.org/browse/SERVER-14070",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:42843a0fb53b621a6746257a57593c33750ae5de7d8d226af966d114de4eeb63",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:b62da981fbd32f8cd29029bb735ec74ee5fa40e3a0eb392d36a52cf416ec4b78"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A range predicate does not universally disqualify index-provided sorting. For {a:1,b:1}, find({a:{$gt:4}}).sort({a:1,b:1}) retains index order; the difficulty is skipping a varying preceding key while sorting a later key alone. (high confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/sort-results-with-indexes.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:c734895de4202c4b6aead763038155748fd173cefa24f732a911191b5e337dc4",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:2f1417076fec0e6138a965334261866c7320909f345885962c8bd656302cc1de"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://www.percona.com/blog/mongodb-index-usage-and-mongodb-explain-part-1/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "When $in is used with a sort and has fewer than 201 elements, the elements are expanded and then merged in the sort order specified for the index using a SORT_MERGE stage. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/equality-sort-range-guideline.md",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/equality-sort-range-guideline.md",
          "note": null,
